IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `types`

§ `Directive (EU) 2015/2366 — refund deadlines and liability allocation`

-- ===========================================================================
-- ⚠  SECONDARY SOURCE — READ NOTES.md §3 BEFORE RELYING ON THIS MODULE.
--
-- FinMont asked for three things: the SCA eligibility check, refund
-- obligation deadlines, and the chargeback liability shift. Only the FIRST is
-- in the document supplied with this job. Commission Delegated Regulation
-- (EU) 2018/389 contains no refund deadline and no liability rule at all: it
-- is a regulatory technical standard on authentication and communication.
--
-- The refund deadlines and the liability shift are in the PARENT INSTRUMENT,
-- Directive (EU) 2015/2366 (PSD2), Title IV, Chapter 4 (Arts 71 to 77) and
-- Chapter 5 (Arts 88 to 93). That text was NOT supplied. This module is
-- therefore encoded from the Directive as the encoder understands it, and
-- every rule in it is marked
--
--     UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for this Article
--
-- The subject's status in subject.json is "draft" for exactly this reason.
-- Deposit the Directive's text in the source bundle and re-verify each @ref
-- before this module goes near a production decision.
--
-- A note on the word "chargeback". Chargeback is a CARD SCHEME mechanism —
-- Visa and Mastercard rulebooks — not an EU-law one. EU law supplies the
-- liability ALLOCATION that the scheme rules then operationalise: PSD2
-- Art 74(2) is the SCA liability shift. Scheme chargeback reason codes and
-- their presentment windows (including the long delayed-delivery windows the
-- travel industry lives on) are contractual and vary by scheme, region and
-- release. They are NOT encoded here and MUST NOT be invented: FinMont
-- supplies them from its own scheme rulebooks. NOTES.md §6 sets out the seam.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `Business days — PSD2 Article 4(37)`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for this Article
-- Art 4(37) defines a business day by reference to whether the relevant
-- provider "is open for business as required for the execution of a payment
-- transaction". That is provider-specific and calendar-specific.
--
-- MODELLED HERE as Monday to Friday only. Public holidays are NOT modelled;
-- see register entry AR-09. A deadline computed here is therefore the
-- EARLIEST possible one, and a real calendar can only push it later.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 4(37) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `start date` IS A DATE
    , `business days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`business days after` MEANS
    IF   `business days` AT MOST 0
    THEN `start date`
    ELSE IF   `is weekend` next
         THEN `business days after` next `business days`
         ELSE `business days after` next (`business days` MINUS 1)
    WHERE
        next MEANS `start date` PLUS 1

GIVEN `start date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the end of the following business day after` MEANS
    `business days after` `start date` 1


§§ `Article 71 — Notification and rectification of unauthorised transactions`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for this Article
-- Art 71(1): the user obtains rectification only if it notifies without undue
-- delay on becoming aware, and in any event no later than 13 months after the
-- debit date.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 71(1) — UNVERIFIED"
`months to notify an unauthorised transaction` MEANS 13

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 71(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `date the amount was debited` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`deadline to notify an unauthorised transaction` MEANS
    `add months` `date the amount was debited` `months to notify an unauthorised transaction`

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 71(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim was notified in time` IF
    (DATE_SERIAL (c's `date the payer notified the payment service provider`))
        AT MOST (DATE_SERIAL (`deadline to notify an unauthorised transaction`
                                 (c's `date the amount was debited`)))


§§ `Article 73 — Refund of an unauthorised payment transaction`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for this Article
-- Art 73(1): the payer's provider refunds the amount "immediately, and in any
-- event no later than by the end of the following business day" after noting
-- or being notified of the transaction, restores the account to the state it
-- would have been in, and gives a credit value date no later than the debit
-- date.
--
-- Second subparagraph: that deadline is suspended where the provider has
-- reasonable grounds to suspect fraud AND communicates those grounds in
-- writing to the relevant national authority.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 73(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A DATE
`deadline to refund an unauthorised transaction` MEANS
    `the end of the following business day after`
        (c's `date the payment service provider became aware or was notified`)

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 73(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the immediate refund obligation is engaged` IF
        `the claim was notified in time` c
    AND NOT c's `the payer acted fraudulently`
    AND NOT c's `the provider has reasonable grounds to suspect fraud and has notified the national authority in writing`

-- Art 73(1) third subparagraph: the credit value date is no later than the
-- date on which the amount was debited.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 73(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A DATE
`latest permitted value date for the refund credit` MEANS
    c's `date the amount was debited`


§§ `Article 74 — Payer liability, and the strong customer authentication shift`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for this Article
-- Art 74(1): by way of derogation from Art 73 the payer may be obliged to
-- bear losses from a lost, stolen or misappropriated instrument up to EUR 50.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(1) — UNVERIFIED"
`article 74 payer liability cap in euro` MEANS 50

-- Art 74(1) second subparagraph: the cap does not apply at all where
-- (a) the loss was not detectable to the payer before the payment (except
--     where the payer acted fraudulently); or
-- (b) the loss was caused by an act or omission of an employee, agent or
--     branch of a provider, or of an outsourcee.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the article 74(1) cap is disapplied in the payer favour` IF
        (    c's `the loss was not detectable to the payer before the payment`
         AND NOT c's `the payer acted fraudulently` )
    OR  c's `the loss was caused by an act or omission of the provider or its agent or outsourcee`

-- Art 74(1) third subparagraph: the payer bears ALL of the losses where it
-- acted fraudulently, or failed its Art 69 obligations with intent or gross
-- negligence. The EUR 50 cap then falls away.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer bears the whole loss under article 74(1)` IF
        c's `the payer acted fraudulently`
    OR  c's `the payer failed article 69 obligations with intent or gross negligence`

-- Art 74(3): no financial consequences for the payer after it has notified
-- the loss under Art 69(1)(b), nor at all where the provider failed to
-- provide means of notification at all times — in each case unless the payer
-- acted fraudulently.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(3) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `article 74(3) relieves the payer entirely` IF
        NOT c's `the payer acted fraudulently`
    AND (    c's `the transaction occurred after the payer notified loss under article 69(1)(b)`
          OR c's `the provider failed to supply means of notification at all times` )

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art 74(2) — THE LIABILITY SHIFT. This is the provision FinMont means when
-- it says "chargeback liability shift", and the only one EU law supplies.
--
--   "Where the payer's payment service provider does not require strong
--    customer authentication, the payer shall not bear any financial losses
--    unless the payer has acted fraudulently. Where the payee or the payee's
--    payment service provider fails to accept strong customer authentication,
--    it shall refund the financial damage caused to the payer's payment
--    service provider."
--
-- Two distinct movements, and they are often conflated:
--   (i)  issuer side — no SCA required by the payer's provider: the payer
--        bears nothing (absent payer fraud);
--   (ii) acceptance side — the payee or its provider did not accept SCA: that
--        party refunds the payer's provider.
-- Limb (ii) can bite even where limb (i) does not.
-- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(2) first sentence — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer bears no loss because SCA was not required` IF
        NOT c's `strong customer authentication was required by the payer payment service provider`
    AND NOT c's `the payer acted fraudulently`

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74(2) second sentence — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payee side must refund the payer payment service provider` IF
    c's `the payee or the payee payment service provider failed to accept strong customer authentication`

-- The allocation, taken in the Directive's own order of precedence: total
-- payer fault first (Art 74(1) third subparagraph), then the reliefs, then
-- the capped residue.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74 — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A `Liability Bearer`
`who bears the loss` MEANS
    IF   `the payer bears the whole loss under article 74(1)` c
    THEN `the payer bears the whole loss`
    ELSE IF   `the payee side must refund the payer payment service provider` c
         THEN `the payee or the payment service provider of the payee bears the loss`
         ELSE IF      `the payer bears no loss because SCA was not required` c
                   OR `article 74(3) relieves the payer entirely` c
                   OR `the article 74(1) cap is disapplied in the payer favour` c
                   OR NOT c's `arose from a lost stolen or misappropriated payment instrument`
              THEN `the payment service provider of the payer bears the loss`
              ELSE `the payer bears up to the article 74 cap`

-- The euro figure the payer actually bears, given that allocation.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 74 — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN c IS AN `Unauthorised Transaction Claim`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`amount the payer bears in euro` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who bears the loss` c
    WHEN `the payer bears the whole loss`      THEN c's `amount in euro`
    WHEN `the payer bears up to the article 74 cap` THEN
        min (c's `amount in euro`) `article 74 payer liability cap in euro`
    WHEN `the payment service provider of the payer bears the loss`       THEN 0
    WHEN `the payee or the payment service provider of the payee bears the loss` THEN 0


§§ `Articles 76 and 77 — Refunds for transactions initiated by or through a payee`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for these Articles
-- Art 77(1): the payer may request the refund for 8 weeks from the date on
-- which the funds were debited.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(1) — UNVERIFIED"
`weeks to request a payee initiated refund` MEANS 8

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `date the funds were debited` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`deadline for the payer to request a payee initiated refund` MEANS
    `date the funds were debited`
        PLUS (`weeks to request a payee initiated refund` TIMES `Days in a week`)

-- Art 77(2): within 10 business days of receiving the request the provider
-- must either refund in full or give a justification for refusing and name
-- the redress bodies.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(2) — UNVERIFIED"
`business days to refund or justify refusal` MEANS 10

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(2) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `date the payer requested the refund` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`deadline to refund or justify refusal` MEANS
    `business days after` `date the payer requested the refund`
                          `business days to refund or justify refusal`

-- Art 76(3)(b): the framework contract may exclude the refund right only
-- where consent was given directly to the provider AND, where applicable,
-- information was made available at least 4 weeks before the due date.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 76(3) — UNVERIFIED"
`weeks of advance information required for the article 76(3) exclusion` MEANS 4

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 76(3) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN r IS A `Payee Initiated Refund Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the framework contract validly excludes the refund right` IF
        r's `the framework contract excludes the refund right on those grounds`
    AND r's `consent was given directly to the payment service provider`
    AND r's `information on the transaction was made available at least 4 weeks before the due date`

@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN r IS A `Payee Initiated Refund Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the refund was requested within the eight week window` IF
        (DATE_SERIAL (r's `date the payer requested the refund`))
            AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL (r's `date the funds were debited`))
    AND (DATE_SERIAL (r's `date the payer requested the refund`))
            AT MOST (DATE_SERIAL (`deadline for the payer to request a payee initiated refund`
                                     (r's `date the funds were debited`)))

-- Art 76(1): the conditional right. Both (a) and (b) must hold, and the payer
-- bears the burden of proving them at the provider's request.
-- Art 76(1) fourth subparagraph: for a SEPA direct debit the right is
-- UNCONDITIONAL, and Art 77(2) second subparagraph removes the provider's
-- power to refuse it.
-- Art 76(2): currency-exchange movement at the agreed reference rate cannot
-- support condition (b).
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 76(1)–(3) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN r IS A `Payee Initiated Refund Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer is entitled to a refund under article 76` IF
        r's `the transaction was authorised`
    AND r's `initiated by or through the payee`
    AND `the refund was requested within the eight week window` r
    AND IF   r's `is a SEPA direct debit within Regulation (EU) No 260/2012`
        THEN TRUE
        ELSE     NOT `the framework contract validly excludes the refund right` r
             AND r's `the authorisation did not specify the exact amount`
             AND r's `the amount exceeded what the payer could reasonably have expected`
             AND NOT r's `the payer relies only on currency exchange reasons at the agreed reference rate`

-- Art 77(2) second subparagraph: the power to refuse does not exist for the
-- unconditional SEPA direct debit right.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 77(2) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN r IS A `Payee Initiated Refund Claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the provider may refuse and justify instead of refunding` IF
    NOT r's `is a SEPA direct debit within Regulation (EU) No 260/2012`


§§ `Articles 89, 90 and 92 — Non-execution, payment initiation services, recourse`

-- UNVERIFIED — no source text supplied for these Articles
-- Art 89(1): where the payer's provider is liable for a non-executed,
-- defectively executed or late executed transaction it refunds "without undue
-- delay" and restores the account; and regardless of liability it must, on
-- request, immediately trace the transaction free of charge. "Without undue
-- delay" is not a countable period and is deliberately left unquantified
-- here; see register entry AR-10.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 89(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `the payer provider can prove the payee provider received the amount` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer payment service provider is liable for correct execution` IF
    NOT `the payer provider can prove the payee provider received the amount`

-- Art 90(2): in the payment-initiation case the account servicing provider
-- refunds on the SAME clock as Art 73(1) — immediately, and in any event no
-- later than by the end of the following business day.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 90(2) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `date the account servicing provider was notified` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`deadline for the account servicing provider to refund a PIS transaction` MEANS
    `the end of the following business day after` `date the account servicing provider was notified`

-- Art 92(1): where a provider's liability under Art 73 or Art 89 is
-- attributable to another provider or to an intermediary, that party
-- compensates the first for losses incurred or sums paid. This is the recourse
-- leg FinMont needs when it sits between the acquirer and the airline.
@ref "Directive (EU) 2015/2366, Art 92(1) — UNVERIFIED"
GIVEN `liability arose under article 73 or article 89`      IS A BOOLEAN
    , `attributable to another provider or an intermediary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a right of recourse arises under article 92` IF
        `liability arose under article 73 or article 89`
    AND `attributable to another provider or an intermediary`


